}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by ${X}^{T},$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\n{X}^{T}\\,X\\overset{^}{\\beta }={X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X}^{T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\beta }=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X}^{T}X)^{-1}$ and ${X}^{T}y$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}38 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}44 \\\\ 250\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}17.2 \\\\ -1.4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the line $y=17.2-1.4x.$\n\nFinally, to predict the selling price of a car of the same model that is $10$ years old, we substitute $x=10$ into the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =17.2-1.4(10) \\\\  & =3.2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, according to the model, the sale price will be $$3\\,200.$\n\n**Note**: Since we're extrapolating ($x=10$ is not within the range of the original set of $x$-values), our result *may* be unreliable." 13194139535930 true] [17592186375070 1000 #edb/ref 17592186375071 13194139535930 true] [17592186375070 1000 #edb/ref 17592186375072 13194139535930 true] [17592186375070 1000 #edb/ref 17592186375073 13194139535930 true] [17592186375070 1000 #edb/ref 17592186375074 13194139535930 true] [17592186375070 1000 #edb/ref 17592186375075 13194139535930 true] [17592186375070 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535930 true] [17592186375070 1041 "selection" 13194139535930 true] [17592186375070 1104 #edb/ref 17592186375074 13194139535930 true] [17592186375070 1110 #uuid "98bcbbb1-f12c-5d23-9b4c-213292e8d0cc" 13194139535930 true] [17592186375071 1034 "$$4\\,400$" 13194139535930 true] [17592186375071 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535930 true] [175